After analyzing the code, the problem was that the `values` function was not correctly handling the order of the readings in the buffer. The function was pushing the readings in the order they were stored in the buffer, but the buffer was storing the readings in the order they were added, with the oldest reading being overwritten first.

The fix was to change the `values` function to return the readings in the correct order, oldest first. This was done by changing the loop in the `values` function to iterate from the head of the buffer to the end of the buffer, and then wrap around to the beginning of the buffer if necessary.

Here is the fix:

```rust
     /// The readings, oldest first.
     pub fn values(&self) -> Vec<i64> {
         let mut out = Vec::with_capacity(self.len);
         for i in 0..self.len {
             out.push(self.buf[(self.head + i) % self.cap]);
         }
         out
     }
```

